Yes—if you are running a self-hosted database VPS in ADT, it really is that simple. You just install Valkey on that database VPS, bind it to your private VPC IP with a password, open port 6379 to your webservers, and update Joomla's configuration.php.
Here is the exact 4-step checklist to wire it together:

1. Install Valkey on the Database VPS

On Ubuntu 24.04+ or Debian 13+ (or via the official Valkey repository on older LTS releases):
bash
apt update && apt install -y valkey
Use code with caution.

2. Configure /etc/valkey/valkey.conf

Set it to listen on your database VPS's private VPC IP (e.g., 10.0.0.2), cap its memory so it never crowds your SQL database, and require authentication:
ini
bind 127.0.0.1 10.0.0.2
protected-mode yes
requirepass YOUR_STRONG_VALKEY_PASSWORD

maxmemory 2gb
maxmemory-policy volatile-lru
save ""
appendonly no
Use code with caution.
Restart Valkey to apply:
bash
systemctl enable --now valkey-server
Use code with caution.

3. Open Port 6379 on the Database VPS Firewall

Allow TCP port 6379 only from your internal VPC subnet (10.0.0.0/24) or your autoscaling webserver IPs—never to 0.0.0.0/0:
bash
ufw allow from 10.0.0.0/24 to any port 6379 proto tcp
Use code with caution.
(If you use a Linode Cloud Firewall on the database VPS, add the same inbound rule for TCP 6379 restricted to your VPC CIDR).

4. Wire Up Joomla on the Webservers

Ensure the PHP Redis extension is installed on your webserver image (apt install -y php-redis), then add these settings to Joomla's configuration.php (inside your adt-webserver-scripts configuration template):
php
// Object & Page Cache via Valkey
public $caching = 1;
public $cache_handler = 'redis';
public $redis_persist = true;
public $redis_server_host = '10.0.0.2';
public $redis_server_port = 6379;
public $redis_server_auth = 'YOUR_STRONG_VALKEY_PASSWORD';
public $redis_server_db = 0;

// Shared User Sessions via Valkey (DB 1 keeps sessions isolated from cache)
public $session_handler = 'redis';
public $session_redis_persist = true;
public $session_redis_server_host = '10.0.0.2';
public $session_redis_server_port = 6379;
public $session_redis_server_auth = 'YOUR_STRONG_VALKEY_PASSWORD';
public $session_redis_server_db = 1;
Use code with caution.
(Separating db = 0 for cache and db = 1 for sessions ensures that clearing Joomla's cache in the admin panel won't accidentally log out your active users).
If you'd like to automate this cleanly in your ADT repositories, let me know:
• Which OS version (e.g., Ubuntu 24.04 or Debian 12/13) your ADT templates use
• Whether you want the exact template variable placeholders to drop into adt-database-scripts and adt-webserver-scripts
I can tailor the exact script lines for your fork.
